WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.910
<v Instructor>So now let's talk about Amazon MQ.</v>

2
00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.340
So we know about SQS and SNS

3
00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.080
and they're cloud native services

4
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.980
because they are proprietary protocols from AWS,

5
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.590
they use their own sets of APIs.

6
00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.340
But if you were running traditional application on-premises

7
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:22.340
you may use open protocols such as MQTT, AMQP, STOMP,

8
00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:27.390
Openwire, WSS, and when you're migrating your application

9
00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:30.330
to the cloud, you may not want to re-engineer

10
00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:34.680
your application to use the SQS and the SNS protocols

11
00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:35.910
or APIs.

12
00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:39.090
So instead you want to use the traditional protocols

13
00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:42.330
you used to, such as MQTT, AMQP, and so on.

14
00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.850
So for this, we can use Amazon MQ.

15
00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:46.830
So Amazon MQ is very simple,

16
00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:49.290
it's a managed message broker service

17
00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:54.090
for two technologies for RabbitMQ and for ActiveMQ.

18
00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.030
So RabbitMQ and ActiveMQ are for example,

19
00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.390
on premises technologies that provide you access

20
00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:03.060
to the open protocols I just mentioned.

21
00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.890
So then we can get a managed versions

22
00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:08.850
of these brokers onto the cloud thanks to Amazon MQ.

23
00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.310
So as such, you can understand the implications.

24
00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:15.630
First of all, Amazon MQ doesn't scale as much as SQS or SNS,

25
00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:18.540
which have infinite scaling.

26
00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.550
And because Amazon MQ runs on servers,

27
00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:22.410
you may have server issues.

28
00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.960
And so you can run Multi-AZ setup

29
00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.350
with a failover if you want to be highly available.

30
00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:31.860
Also, Amazon MQ comes with both a queue feature,

31
00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.900
so it looks like SQS,

32
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.510
and topic features, it looks like SNS,

33
00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.733
as part of a single broker.

34
00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.720
<v ->So for Amazon MQ, what can you migrate from?</v>

35
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:46.200
Well, if you have IBM MQ, TIBCO EMS, RabbitMQ,

36
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.880
or Apache ActiveMQ, they can all be migrated to Amazon MQ

37
00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.670
thanks to the compatibility in the protocols.

38
00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.340
So this is a blog you can read on the AWS blog,

39
00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.470
which just shows that you can migrate

40
00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:03.300
from IBM WebSphere MQ to AWS directly,

41
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:07.830
by leveraging Amazon MQ instead of IBM WebSphere MQ.

42
00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:08.760
And this is quite interesting

43
00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:11.250
because in this exam you are definitely asked

44
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:15.000
to consider migrations, and think of the right tool

45
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.730
for the right type of migration you want to do.

46
00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:18.960
So that's it for this lecture.

47
00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.600
Very, very simple for Amazon MQ, but hopefully important,

48
00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.550
and I will see you in the next lecture.

